<speak version="1.0" xml:lang="en-US" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xmlns:amazon="http://amazon.com/schemas/ssml-1.0">
<amazon:domain name="conversational">

<mark name="intro_start"/>
Most smart-home skills handle one command at a time.
<break time="300ms"/>
Ask a real household assistant something bigger —
<break time="200ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">"what's due this week, and can you reorder anything running low?"</emphasis>
<break time="300ms"/>
and it falls apart.
<break time="400ms"/>
This is HomeOps: a self-hosted
<say-as interpret-as="characters">MCP</say-as>
server built for the Alexa+ track,
that gives Alexa+ real household tools it can chain together in one turn.

<break time="500ms"/>

<mark name="feature_tasks"/>
Here's a household's open tasks.
<break time="200ms"/>
Ask HomeOps to add one, and it fairly assigns it based on who already has the lightest load
<break time="200ms"/>
— no one has to argue about whose turn it is.

<break time="500ms"/>

<mark name="feature_reorder"/>
Now the shopping list is running low on a few items.
<break time="300ms"/>
When Alexa+ tries to reorder one, HomeOps doesn't just spend money silently.
<break time="200ms"/>
It uses <say-as interpret-as="characters">MCP</say-as> elicitation
to have Alexa+ ask the user directly, in real time, before the order goes through.
<break time="400ms"/>
That's the protocol-correct way to gate a spending action — not a hidden confirm flag.

<break time="500ms"/>

<mark name="feature_budget"/>
Log an expense that pushes a category over its weekly budget,
<break time="200ms"/>
and HomeOps flags it immediately.
<break time="300ms"/>
Ask for the week's story instead of raw numbers,
<break time="200ms"/>
and it generates a natural-language summary
using <say-as interpret-as="characters">MCP</say-as> sampling
— asking the connected model to write it, with no
<say-as interpret-as="characters">API</say-as> key of its own.

<break time="500ms"/>

<mark name="feature_briefing"/>
And this is the compound tool:
<break time="200ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">run morning briefing</emphasis>
chains tasks, fair assignment, low stock, and budget status into a single call,
<break time="200ms"/>
then narrates all of it back in one breath.
<break time="300ms"/>
One request. One conversation turn. The whole household, handled.

<break time="600ms"/>

<mark name="opensource_start"/>
For the open source mini-challenge, we built
<emphasis level="reduced">friction-log-toolkit</emphasis>
<break time="200ms"/>
— a tiny command-line tool that captures a friction point the moment it happens,
<break time="200ms"/>
instead of trying to remember it an hour before a deadline.
<break time="300ms"/>
One command while you build.
<break time="200ms"/>
One export at submission time.

<break time="600ms"/>

<mark name="close_start"/>
Every tool here is real and tested end to end —
<break time="200ms"/>
no stubs, no mocked protocol calls.
<break time="300ms"/>
HomeOps, for the Alexa+ track.
<break time="200ms"/>
Built, shipped, and shaped for the household that actually runs itself.

</amazon:domain>
</speak>
